Optimal Solution - Determine if all courses can be completed using topological sorting and indegree tracking.
                  If there’s a cycle in the prerequisite graph, Kahn’s algorithm won’t process all courses.

class Solution {
    public boolean canFinish(int numCourses, int[][] prerequisites) {
        // Adjacency list representing course dependencies
        List<List<Integer>> graph = new ArrayList<>();
        // inDegree[i] = number of prerequisites for course i
        int[] inDegree = new int[numCourses];
        // Initialize graph
        for (int i = 0; i < numCourses; i++) {
            graph.add(new ArrayList<>());
        }
        // Build the graph and indegree array
        for (int[] pre : prerequisites) {
            graph.get(pre[1]).add(pre[0]); // edge: pre[1] -> pre[0]
            inDegree[pre[0]]++;
        }
        // Queue for BFS (Kahn's Algorithm)
        Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();
        // Add courses with no prerequisites
        for (int i = 0; i < numCourses; i++) {
            if (inDegree[i] == 0) {
                queue.offer(i);
            }
        }
        int count = 0; // number of courses completed
        // Process courses in topological order
        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            int curr = queue.poll();
            count++;
            // Reduce indegree of dependent courses
            for (int next : graph.get(curr)) {
                inDegree[next]--;
                if (inDegree[next] == 0)
                    queue.offer(next);
            }
        }
        // If all courses are completed, return true
        return count == numCourses;
    }
}

Time Complexity :- O(V + E).
Space Complexity :- O(V + E).
